            if (New_Return)
                Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}
```

As you can observe, we created unit tests for each of the ArrayList methods that are not part of the System.Collections.ArrayList contract. This includes tests for the Clear, Contains, Count, IndexOf, and LastIndexOf methods. The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute is used to specify that a parameter of the method under test should be treated as the target under test, effectively isolating its effects.

Each test checks the Precondition and Post condition of each method. If the precondition is true (we assume that it's always true, as stated in AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true)), then we are observing the values before and after executing the method. After that, we are observing the return value of the method call (new vs old), and then checking the Post condition based on the observed values. For instance, in the case of the Contains method, we are checking if the new last index of x is greater or equal the old last index of x.

This way, each of our tests checks the assumptions defined in the contract using the unit tests we have designed. It does not explicitly validate the ArrayList class against its contract, but rather it does a "unit testing by contract". This approach helps in identifying deviations between the behavior of the class under test and the behavior according to the contract, ensuring that the class really behaves according to its specifications. It thus covers the basic contract tests, i.e., IsEmpty, Count, Contains, IndexOf, and LastIndexOf. For more complex contracts, a deep analysis and additional code would be required.

However, it should also be noted that this Pex run does not have any assertions, because Pex is not a unit testing tool, but a testing guidance tool -- it merely generates data for test runs. It won't validate any tests; it only helps generate tests based on data it observes. It is common practice to manually review the test methods that are generated and to extend them with explicit assertions according to a testing strategy. This can be done in addition, where one can also cover edge cases and complex test scenarios and write tests for methods that are not defined within the contract (like Insert, Remove, RemoveAt, CopyTo, SyncRoot, etc.). This requires writing additional test code that is aligned with the strategy of a good unit test.  


### Test Automation Framework

To apply test automation, a framework would be utilized to automate the unit tests and to manage the execution. An advantage of using a framework is that it helps maintain, organize, and manage the execution of tests, as well as to ensure that the tests are robust and can be re-run multiple times without affecting the results. The framework also provides the ability to analyze and report the test results in a uniform format. Some common test automation frameworks include Selenium, JUnit, TestNG, Mocha, and Karma.

In the case of Pex unit tests, since it is a testing guidance tool and does not perform actual test executions, the Pex tests could be included in a separate testing suite that invokes Pex automatically (either as part of the build process or as a separate testing process) and then compares the result with the expected values. This can be an important consideration in practice, especially for complex or high-risk code, where the results need to be verified thoroughly.

Moreover, it would be beneficial to integrate automated testing with a CI/CD (Continuous Integration/Continuous Delivery) system, so that tests are automatically performed whenever changes are made to the code base. This can significantly enhance the efficiency and reliability of the code development process.


To Conclude
Test Automations can be applied to the ArrayListTest by creating unit test methods and using the Test Automation Framework. In this case, the Automated Unit Testing Framework (like Pex) will be used to generate the unit tests and then the test cases can be run using a framework like NUnit, which is a popular testing framework. Additionally, the automated tests would be integrated into the CI/CD pipeline to ensure tests are run automatically whenever changes are made to the code to improve code quality as well as make the code maintenance process more automated and efficient. 

