A: Your test method is written in a way that it is dependent on pre-conditions (presumably that they will hold true) and might fail unintentionally if those pre-conditions do not hold. This is a common pitfall when writing property based tests or fuzzing tests.
AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

In this situation, it's better to add a pre-condition check to ensure the pre-conditions are indeed true before performing any operations. 
Additionally, the test is currently only focusing on the return value of the Contains method and nothing else. Testing if a list's count, IndexOf or LastIndexOf remain unchanged or increase is also a good idea.
For the purpose of this test, if the returned value of Contains is true, the list's IndexOf should not decrease when x is added again to the list and LastIndexOf should increase.
Also, rather than using true as a pre-condition, it would be better to consider some edge cases:


*

*An empty list 

*A list containing a single element x which is added and removed,

*A list containing multiple elements and one of them happens to be 'x'

*A large list


It might be beneficial to add some pre-conditions to reflect these edge cases.
Also, I suggest using the pexSettings attribute [PexMethod(ExcludePaths = new[] { ".*" })] to prevent Pex from running the test on all methods in the process - this can considerably reduce the testing time.
It's also worth considering the potential for unexpected behavior when the input contains duplicate elements, like if you add the same item 'x' multiple times to or from the list.
Overall, there's a lot to consider when designing your tests and depending on the method being tested, the testing strategy might need to be adjusted accordingly.
Regarding your Pex issues, consider reaching out to the Pex UserVoice forum or the Pex MSDN forum for questions and for more guidance.
I hope it helps, and I wish you the best of luck with your testing endeavors.
Gilad

A: After examining your code, it seems that you have not provided the correct post-condition for the Contains method in your test. In your code snippets, you have only written the pre-condition (an assume and an expectation for the index of x), but no post-condition.

Your Pex test is currently based on an assumption in form of an "AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);" statement, which is incorrect. Pex works on the principles of "property-based testing" where it expects to find properties such as 'correctness', 'safety', 'liveness', 'termination', 'soundness', etc. It's basically looking for behaviors that are expected in any 'good' implementation.

The problem with your test is that the post-condition has not been established. Therefore, it will be quite unreliable and may not be meaningful or accurate, especially in the real world where bugs can't be assumed to be there just to be found.

Unfortunately, as an AI, I'm limited to pointing out what the problem might be and making assumptions about your intentions. If you need more detailed guidance on this I recommend discussing it directly with one of the developers or Pex professionals who can provide precise feedback based on the code context and problems you've presented. I'm sorry for any potential inconvenience and appreciate your understanding.
