As the first assertion passed and the second one failed with the message:
System.IndexOutOfRangeException: Index was outside the bounds of the array.
The issue seems to be that when we add an item to the list and the capacity of the list is reached, more memory is allocated in a larger block, and the old array is replaced with the new one. But the reference to the new array still points to the old one. The actual data in the new array is unknown.

I also tried the same with List<T>, but faced the same issue.
As you can see, every time we try to run basic test around the contains method, we face these kind of issues. I tested these scenarios with other methods as well like add, remove, indexOf, and they worked perfectly.

Is there a way to test these scenarios effectively using MSTest or any other testing tools without encountering these kind of issues? Please advice.

A: This exception is thrown because you are accessing an index in the ArrayList that doesn't exist.  An ArrayList dynamically resize to accommodate new items inserted. Once the list has to reallocate, the old references are no longer valid, and you encounter an IndexOutOfRangeException.
Let's imagine that you have an array of 5 elements and you try accessing element at index 5. This operation is not allowed because the element at index 5 doesn't exist. 
The same principle applies to your case. After an insert operation, when the list is resized, you can't trust the list to remember the old values. 
The problem doesn't come from testing, it's from the .NET Framework ArrayList class behavior.

As an alternative, you could consider using a List object instead of the ArrayList. The List<T> class provides the Contains method and it doesn't suffer from the same issue. 

In unit testing, testing for expected exceptions is done when you know that calling a method will throw a certain exception. In your case, since Contains() is expected to throw the IndexOutOfRangeException, the test should check for this. Here's a sample unit test code.

```csharp
[--------------------	    Assert.AreEqual(New_Ret, Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the return value is false, new list's index of x is -1
	    Assert.AreEqual(New_Ret, New_arrListIndexOfX == -1);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the return value is false, new list's count is = old list's count
	    Assert.AreEqual(New_Ret, !New_Ret || New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);
        }
    }
}
```

